  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe proposed approach combines dimensionality reduction with coordinate-fixed optimization to tackle the minimum overlap integral problem. This differs from previous methods by:\n\n- Utilizing a step function representation where the function values are constrained to [0,1] and the total integral is fixed at 0.5 (due to dx=2.0/n_points).\n- Employing input coordinate normalization and a parameterized batch to balance global search and local optimization.\n- Using a block-evaluation method that minimizes the maximum correlation without explicitly using gradient-based optimization.\n\nThe strategy addresses the need to escape local minima found in gradient-based methods by introducing:\n\n- A block-coordinate optimization that varies only a few parameters at a time\n- A novel fitness score that considers both the maximum correlation and shape preservation\n- Dynamic block selection based on current problem state\n\nHowever, computational complexity increases with higher resolutions due to longer correlation operations. The method relies on balance between global search through block variation and local refinement through precise cross-validated parameters.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nThe optimization variables are:\n- A real-valued array h \u2208 [0,1]^n with integral constraint \u2211h[i] = n/2\n- The full cross-correlation is computed via mode=\"full\" operation\n- The objective C5_max is the maximum of (cross-correlation array) at all lags\n- The constraint set is defined by 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, and \u2211h[i] = n/2\n\nThe objective function is:\nC5 = max_k (integral of h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx over [0,2-k])\n\nThe fitness score incorporates:\n- Penalty terms for values outside [0,1]\n- A block-magnitude constraint to maintain flip resolution\n- Shape preservation coefficients\n\nThe transformation involves mapping optimized block values back to the original coordinate system, preserving boundary conditions.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nThe algorithm proceeds in three main stages:\n\nStep 1: Initialization\n- If 'initial_h_values' is available, use it; else generate random with adjustment to meet integral constraint\n- Normalize all coordinates to [0,2\u03c0] domain for analysis\n- Set initial block boundaries and fitness coefficients\n\nStep 2: Optimization loop\n- For each generation:\n  - Select block index uniformly (or strategically)\n  - Optimize block parameters with coordinate constraints\n  - Check boundary violations and adjust if necessary\n  - Project entire function to meet global integral constraint\n  - Evaluate fitness with component transformation\n\nStep 3: Output selection\n- After complete optimization:\n  - Re-evaluate the final candidate\n  - Schedule compare cross-validation with alternative resolution levels\n  - Return the best found solution with n_points set to 320\n\nKey operations:\n- np.clip() for fulfillment\n- project_h() for integral adjustment\n- np.diff() for boundary condition checks\n- coordinate mapping via standard basis vectors\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\nCore parameters:\n- population_size = 2048\n- iterations = 40\n- boundary_padding = 0.5\n- adjustment_factor = 0.9\n- max_tuning_attempts = 3\n\nFor advanced variations:\n- 'coordinate_flipping' (variation 1) forces boundary morphology\n- 'block_magnitude' (variation 2) regulates search extent\n- 'shape_preservation' (variation 3) maintains persistent patterns\n\nThese are activated based on fitness performance:\n- Variation 1 used if fitness stagnates\n- Variation 2 applied during 1/3rd of optimization\n- Variation 3 active in noisy environments\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nTime allocation:\n- Initial block warm-up: 5 seconds\n- Optimization core: 90 seconds\n- Fitness evaluation: 10 seconds\n- Final projection: 5 seconds\n\nMemory profile:\n- Minimal storage requirements\n- Single point optimization per block\n- No large arrays stored\n\nFallback mechanisms:\n- If perturbation succeeds: continue with best parameters\n- Else: fall back to previous generation's best\n- Maintain rolling window of top 10 scores\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nCandidate must satisfy:\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i (checked via np.clip)\n2. Integral condition \u2211h[i] = n/2 (verified by project_h)\n3. C5 calculation via np.correlate is finite (defaulted to np.full)\n4. Output block preserves length 320\n\nMetrics returned:\n- h_values: 320-length array for projection\n- c5_bound: float(min_overlap) or np.inf\n- n_points: 320 (verified by output channel)\n\nCritical checks:\n1. All array lengths match domain [0,2]\n2. np.linalg.norm(correlation) < 1e10\n3. Integral projection validates (\u22650.5*(n-1))\n4. Temperature schedule remains below 0.5 \n\nEach variation includes side-channel monitoring for alignment with Erd\u0151s specification and coherence with compatibility protocols. Shape-preserving parameters are updated every 1000th generation via Bayesian annealing schedules.",
